  "result": "There is a drawer in my imaginary house reserved for things that have not yet found their names. Inside are the particular silence after a train leaves, the courage required to knock twice, and the small sadness of discovering that a familiar tree has been cut down during your absence.\n\nThe drawer sticks in damp weather. This seems appropriate. Unnamed things are rarely cooperative. They catch on the edges of ordinary language and refuse to fit beneath convenient labels like happiness or grief. Sometimes I pull too hard, and everything spills across the floor in a jumble of unfinished understanding.\n\nOne morning, I find a new arrival: the feeling of hearing someone laugh in another room and knowing, without seeing them, that they are finally getting better. It looks like a brass key. It is warm enough to suggest that somebody has just held it tightly inside a closed hand.\n\nI consider naming it relief, but relief is too broad, a whole country where this feeling is merely one lit window. Hope is always looking ahead. Gratitude asks for an object. So I set the key beside the others and leave its little mystery intact for one more quiet day.\n\nPerhaps that is what attention means: making room for an experience before deciding what it is. Not every visitor needs a passport. Not every ache needs a diagnosis. Outside, rain begins. The drawer swells shut. I leave it alone and stand listening to water finding the names of ordinary things.",
